WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.277 --> 00:00:02.650
Narrator: In "The Batman,"
Viewers see

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:05.700
the first-ever electric Batmobile.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.980
To get this blue fire look practically,

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.540
the team had to pivot to
electric to avoid fires on set

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.896
and put a jet intake under its hood

00:00:13.896 --> 00:00:16.170
to make it look like it was breathing.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.520
This fits right in with
the Batman franchise's

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.690
commitment to in-camera effects,

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.700
as the films have pulled
off some of the Batmobile's

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.030
most impressive feats with minimal CGI.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:29.370
Here's how six other
memorable stunts came to life.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.040
Christopher Nolan's
version of the Batmobile,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.480
the Tumbler, looks like
a cross between a Hummer,

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.550
a tank, and a Lamborghini.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.750
And because the director
was set on capturing

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.810
the movie's police chase in camera,

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:45.520
this tanklike car had to
be fast and maneuverable.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.180
So the team constructed the Batmobile

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.870
with side-mounted wheels
and no front axle,

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.410
which allowed it to pull
off super-tight turns,

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.716
and designed its six wheels

00:00:53.716 --> 00:00:55.678
to act independently of each other,

00:00:55.678 --> 00:00:57.943
so that one could stop
while the other rotated

00:00:57.943 --> 00:01:00.400
to make the car change
direction on a dime.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.640
That meant this Batmobile could actually

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.430
execute those sharp moves
you see in the movies.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.840
It could also jump six feet in height

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.007
and 60 feet in distance

00:01:10.007 --> 00:01:12.970
and really did run over the
two cop cars in this shot,

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.520
a move the team practiced
before the shoot.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.860
Stunt driver George Cottle

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.730
trained for the police chase for months

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.910
by taking the Batmobile for
laps on a closed racetrack.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:23.819
When it came time to film in Chicago,

00:01:23.819 --> 00:01:25.650
the stunt team tracked the Batmobile

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.810
with a device called the Ultimate Arm,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.470
a high-speed, high-torque
robotic camera crane

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:33.030
that could spin 360 degrees.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.808
Attached to the top of a Mercedes,

00:01:34.808 --> 00:01:37.047
this allowed them to get
car-to-car tracking shots

00:01:37.047 --> 00:01:39.721
at speeds of up to 90 miles per hour.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.280
Despite the Tumbler's impressive specs,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.083
there was one move it couldn't do.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.560
Jump across the rooftops of Gotham.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.872
He's flying on rooftops!

00:01:50.872 --> 00:01:51.850
Narrator: So the crew decided

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.750
to do this stunt with miniatures,

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.270
1:3-scale models of the
Gotham set and Batmobile.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.100
The team replicated key
details of the full-scale car

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.933
on the mini remote-controlled model

00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:03.624
so that the two versions of the Batmobile

00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:06.160
would drive as similarly as possible.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.270
The model needed to drive
at around 35 miles per hour,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.755
which may not sound like much,

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:12.850
but is pretty fast for a miniature.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.900
To match the background
of the miniature footage

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.340
to the location in Chicago,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.060
the crew extensively photographed

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.670
the entire shooting area
and took detailed notes

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.250
on the location's lighting.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:24.370
Tying it all together
were the finishing touches

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.050
added digitally, including
camera lens effects,

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:30.340
CG car headlights, moving
reflections, vapor plumes,

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.050
and debris, like these
shattering roof tiles you see

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.133
as the Batmobile sticks its landing.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.920
After the location shoot in Chicago,

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.610
the team had to tackle another

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.970
seemingly impossible stunt
for the very end of the chase,

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.260
when the Batmobile makes
its dramatic return

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.840
through a waterfall at
the mouth of the Batcave.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.790
They shot the takeoff for real on a field,

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.260
but the landing was filmed
on the Batcave set in the UK.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.570
There, the team made a
full-scale waterfall,

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.250
using 12 water pumps to run

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.220
14,000 gallons of water through the set.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.060
Using huge high-pressure nitrogen cannons,

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.130
they catapulted the over-2½-ton Batmobile

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.720
through the waterfall sans driver.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.200
This was risky, so they
used a restraining cable

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.340
to stop the car right upon landing,

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:19.960
preventing it from hurtling through

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.993
the other end of the
extremely expensive set.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.890
When Christopher Nolan
saw that the Batmobile

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.770
could jump up to 60 feet,
he decided to take it

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.440
a step further in "The Dark Knight."

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.440
The movie's chase would
involve the Batmobile

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.231
jumping over another car,

00:03:36.231 --> 00:03:37.922
intercepting the Joker's grenade,

00:03:37.922 --> 00:03:41.304
and then getting destroyed
in a very spectacular way.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.100
Since this would all
take place in a tunnel,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.350
stunt driver George Cottle

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.690
had to execute the jump perfectly.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:53.610
Too much height, and he'd
hit the concrete above.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:54.918
The stunt team built a ramp

00:03:54.918 --> 00:03:56.368
at just the right height and angle

00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:58.620
that they could tow behind the other car

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.000
and created a big button

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.403
for Cottle to press inside the Batmobile,

00:04:02.403 --> 00:04:04.950
so that he could trigger
the explosion himself.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:06.440
The key was timing it.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.970
He had to wait until the car
was at the top of its arc,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.450
when he just began to feel it come down.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.166
The car exploded from the back,

00:04:13.166 --> 00:04:14.260
and to keep Cottle safe,

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.200
it had explosion-proof gas tanks

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.050
like the kind used on race cars

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.725
so that if the tanks got punctured,

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:21.703
they wouldn't actually explode.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.500
The final installment of
the Dark Knight trilogy

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.050
introduces the Batwing,

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:30.841
which serves as Batman's new ride

00:04:30.841 --> 00:04:32.140
after a bunch of Tumblers

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.530
fall into the hands of Bane's henchmen.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:38.410
This time around, the team had
to make Batman's vehicle fly,

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.500
and because it's a
Christopher Nolan movie,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.970
they had to do it with as
little CGI as possible.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:47.280
The special effects team built
two real versions of the Bat.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.680
Both were massive, at 28
feet long and 17 feet wide.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:52.740
There was a lighter version

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.520
that the team could lift up on cables,

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.510
which were removed in post to
create the illusion of flight,

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.012
then there was a heavier version,

00:04:59.012 --> 00:05:01.640
which weighed 1½ tons and had working guns

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:03.620
that fired via remote control.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.548
The team mounted this Bat on
a truck with a hydraulic crane

00:05:06.548 --> 00:05:08.156
and drove it through the
streets of Pittsburgh

00:05:08.156 --> 00:05:10.305
at 50 miles an hour.

00:05:10.305 --> 00:05:14.140
The VFX team removed the
support car and crane in post.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.110
While the Bat could be
driven in a straight line

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.000
and could even swerve, it wasn't great

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.880
at going around corners at high speeds.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.330
So for those shots where the Bat

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.104
did especially wild maneuvers,

00:05:24.104 --> 00:05:26.590
the VFX team had to
replace the practical Bat

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:28.180
with a digital replica.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.880
Selling it wasn't easy.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.280
Much of "The Dark Knight Rises"

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.040
takes place in the daytime --

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:34.840
unusual for a Batman movie.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.840
That, coupled with the decision
to shoot the film in Imax,

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.140
meant the level of detail on the CG Bat

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.720
had to stand up to a super-high resolution

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.450
under full lighting.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.022
Luckily, the VFX artists

00:05:46.022 --> 00:05:48.605
had the advantage of new technology:

00:05:48.605 --> 00:05:50.713
a physically based ray-tracing system

00:05:50.713 --> 00:05:52.430
partly developed at Pixar.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:53.617
Ray tracing is a technique

00:05:53.617 --> 00:05:55.667
of mapping out all the
rays of light in a scene

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:58.360
as they bounce off different
objects and characters.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.020
Before, artists would've had to grade

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:02.420
the surface of the CG Bat by hand.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.820
Now the software helped
automate that process,

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:06.630
which made it easier to mimic the shadows

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.980
and highlights on every
inch of the real thing.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:13.740
Ben Affleck's 8,800-pound Batmobile

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.850
took over a year to build.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:19.150
So how were they going
to film shots like this?

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.440
The team created not one
but three Batmobiles.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.550
Well, sort of.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.450
There was the real deal
that Affleck drove,

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:28.570
with a full interior cockpit.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:30.070
Then there were two proxy cars,

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:31.970
which they called "smashmobiles" --

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.910
basically stripped-down
Dodge Ram duallies.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:36.700
When any shots called for the Batmobile

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.430
to collide with other objects,

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.420
the filmmakers used one of
the smashmobiles instead,

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.110
shooting on location in
the streets of Detroit.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.683
Actually doing the stunts
with a stand-in car

00:06:46.683 --> 00:06:49.910
gave the VFX artist a
practical starting point,

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:52.070
so when the VFX team digitally swapped

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:54.190
the smashmobile for the Batmobile in post,

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.110
the action looked much more grounded.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.250
It's that commitment to practical methods

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.920
that has brought this car
to life again and again

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.113
and turned it into its very
own character in the films.

00:07:06.352 --> 00:07:07.980
So what do you think?

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.720
Does it come in black?

